---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Weak = `My function draws a target. It takes x and y.
It works well and I am happy with it.`;

const Strong = `drawTarget(x, y, size) draws one target wherever
it is told to. The rings are drawn by a loop that
counts down from 3, and an if inside it colors a
hit target gray instead of red. It is called from
the traversal that draws all the targets, and
again to draw the one under the mouse.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.26: Creative Artifact &mdash; Writing It Down</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.26</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Creative Artifact &mdash; Writing It Down</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Explaining Your Own Code</p>
              <p>What a Good Answer Looks Like</p>
              <p>Looking at Everybody Else's</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Part Nobody Practices</h2>
              <p>You built it. Now write about it.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Part Nobody Practices</h2>
              <p>You built it. Now write about it.</p>
              <p>
                In May you will sit in a room with{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">no computer</b> and answer four written
                questions about a program you wrote months earlier.
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Today is that, at one tenth the size, one week after building
                it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>This Answer Earns Nothing</h2>
              <Code code={Weak} frame="code" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>This Answer Earns Nothing</h2>
              <Code code={Weak} frame="code" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                It is true, it is about the right function, and it says{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">nothing a reader could not have
                guessed from the name.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>This One Does</h2>
              <Code code={Strong} frame="code" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>This One Does</h2>
              <Code code={Strong} frame="code" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                Names the parameters. Says what the loop does and what the
                decision decides.{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Says where it is called from, twice.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Same function. Same program. One of these is worth marks.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Write About What Is There</h2>
              <p>
                Not what you meant to build, not what it would do with another
                week.
              </p>
              <p class="text-sn-theme">
                A small program described precisely beats a big one described
                vaguely.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Then the Gallery Walk</h2>
              <p>
                Everyone's program runs. Everyone else walks around and plays
                them.
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                You are looking for one thing you want to steal and one thing
                you would ask about. Both go on your sheet.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Describe your program's purpose and how it is used</li>
                <li>Explain your own procedure precisely</li>
                <li>Say what you cut, and why</li>
                <li>Look at everyone else's and take something from it</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Documentation</dt>
                  <dd>Writing that explains what a program does and how.</dd>
                  <dt>Functionality</dt>
                  <dd>How a program behaves while it runs, described by what a user does. The exam's word.</dd>
                  <dt>Abstraction</dt>
                  <dd>Using something by its name without needing its details. A function you call is one.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Open your program once, run it, then close
            the editor</b> and write from what you remember. If you get stuck,
            open it again &mdash; but notice that you had to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 1: Purpose and Use</h3>

          <p>Two short paragraphs on the documentation sheet:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Purpose.</b> What the program is for. Not the mechanics
              &mdash; why anyone would run it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Use.</b> What a person does, in order, from launching it to
              finishing. What they press, what they see, how it ends.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 2: Your Procedure</h3>

          <p>
            The graded one. Write about the function you wrote, and cover all
            five:
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Its name and its parameters, and what each parameter is for</li>
            <li>What it does with them</li>
            <li>What the <b>loop</b> inside it repeats, and how many times</li>
            <li>What the <b>decision</b> inside it decides</li>
            <li>The <b>two places</b> it is called from, and why each one calls it</li>
          </ol>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Then paste the function itself underneath. It should be short enough
            to read in one go &mdash; if it is not, say so and explain which
            part matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 3: The Table</h3>

          <p>
            What is in it, what the program does with it, and{" "}
            <b>what would happen if you deleted it.</b>
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            If the honest answer is "not much", say that. An accurate
            description of a weak table is worth more than an invented
            description of a strong one, and it tells you exactly what to fix in
            Quarter 3.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 4: What You Cut</h3>

          <p>Three sentences:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>What you planned that is not in the program</li>
            <li>When you decided to drop it, and why</li>
            <li>What you would build first with another session</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 5: One Bug</h3>

          <p>
            Describe the worst thing that went wrong while building.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">What you saw, what you thought it was, and
            what it actually was.</b>
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            "What you thought it was" is the interesting part and it is the part
            people leave out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 6: The Gallery Walk</h3>

          <p>
            Leave your program running and go and look at everyone else's.{" "}
            <b>Play at least five.</b> For each one you write about, take:
          </p>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Whose</th>
                  <th>One thing it does well</th>
                  <th>One thing you want to know how they did</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
                <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
                <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Ask the second column out loud before the session ends.</b>{" "}
            Everybody in this room solved a problem you did not, and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">this is the cheapest hour of the quarter
            for finding that out.</b>
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Purpose and use, both written</li>
            <li>
              Your procedure described with all five points, and the code
              pasted
            </li>
            <li>The table described honestly, including the delete test</li>
            <li>What you cut, and when you decided</li>
            <li>One bug, including what you thought it was</li>
            <li>At least three programs recorded from the gallery walk</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              You wrote about your program with the editor closed. Say which
              part you could not remember, and what that tells you about writing
              code you will have to explain in May.
            </li>
            <li>
              Somebody else's program did something yours did not. Describe it,
              and say what you think they had to know to build it.
            </li>
            <li>
              This quarter started with a program that printed one line. Name
              the single idea from the last twenty-five sessions that made the
              biggest difference to what you can build, and say why that one.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Submit the documentation sheet and, if you changed anything today,
            the program folder again.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Quarter Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
